ptimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(7{x}^{2}y-2xy+3{y}^{3}-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(7{x}^{2}y-2xy+3{y}^{3}-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}14xy-2y \\\\ 7{x}^{2}-2x+9{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(14xy-2y) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(7{x}^{2}-2x+9{y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(14xy-2y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(7{x}^{2}-2x+9{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}14y & 14x-2 \\\\ 14x-2 & 18y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}14y & 14x-2 \\\\ 14x-2 & 18y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}14xy-2y \\\\ 7{x}^{2}-2x+9{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}14y & 14x-2 \\\\ 14x-2 & 18y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}14xy-2y \\\\ 7{x}^{2}-2x+9{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}14(0) & 14(-1)-2 \\\\ 14(-1)-2 & 18(0)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}14(-1)(0)-2(0) \\\\ 7(-1)^{2}-2(-1)+9(0)^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}0 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{0\\cdot 0-(-16)\\cdot (-16)}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{256}[\\begin{aligned}144 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.438 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 